  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1254 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nThis afternoon through Sunday: Mostly Dry with Warm Conditions\n\nNorthwest to west flow is forecast to prevail through the rest\nof this work week which will keep temperatures fairly steady in\nthe upper 60s to mid 70s each afternoon. Mostly dry conditions \nshould persist as well, outside of the low chance (10-30%) for \nlight rain on Friday morning. A narrow corridor of positive \ntheta-e advection is forecast to move over Iowa towards the \nGreat Lakes on Friday as strong WAA in the lower levels surges \nnortheast towards the Northern High Plains and Upper Midwest. \nSubtle PVA in the upper level flow may aid in the development of\nscattered rain showers, but given the amount of drier air over \nthe region, the anticipation is that not much rainfall should \noccur with this activity.\n\nRidging begins to build further north this upcoming weekend \nincreasing the likelihood of well above average temperatures for \nboth Saturday and Sunday. Ensemble forecast guidance continues\nto be in great agreement that Saturday will be the warmest day \nof this forecast period. The latest 07/13z NBM probabilities \nstill show a strong signal for highs to reach or exceed 80 \ndegrees for most locations across the Driftless Region. In \naddition to the warm conditions, southwest wind will increase on\nSaturday in response to an increasing LLJ at H850 with wind \nspeeds up to 35-45 kts. This should translate to surface wind \nspeeds up to 20-25 mph with gusts upwards of 30-40 mph. NBM \nprobabilities for gusts to reach or exceed 40 mph are the \nhighest across the far western portions of the CWA, closer to \nthe low level jet max, and those probabilities are upwards of \n40-60 percent. Temperatures should decrease by a few degrees on \nSunday along with much less noticeable wind as the ridge moves \noff to the east.\n\nEarly Next Week: Trending Cooler and Rain Chances Return\n\nGlobal model guidance is in good agreement that a trough will\ndig south across the western CONUS early next week but that is \nabout where the agreement ends. How the trough and the \nassociated center of low pressure evolve throughout the rest of \nthe week remains uncertain. In any case, this pattern should \nlead to the potential for cooler conditions along with chances \nfor precipitation. Disturbances out ahead of the trough combined\nwith surging moisture should lead to precipitation chances \nduring the next work week. The main questions right now are just\nwhen are the best chances for precip and how much precipitation\ncould occur with this system. Additionally, multiple tropical \ncyclones may attempt to interact with the troughing pattern \nacross the west and those systems may also have an impact on the\nprecipitation potential. Right now, there is fairly good \nensemble agreement that this could occur in two waves, with the \nfirst being on Monday into Tuesday and the second one being \nduring the later half of the week. Those details will become \nmore clear as we get closer to early next week.",
